In numerous molecular and self-assembled capsules,1-3 cages4-6 and containers,7-10 rates of guest uptake are controlled by the sizes of openings generated by structural linkers,11 and the size of the host-domain itself. In addition, chemical properties of the host domains provide thermodynamic control12 over the uptake of chemically different guests, based on non-covalent interactions ranging from van der Waals forces13,14 to hydrogen bonding, electrostatic effects15 and halogen bonding.16 Guest uptake by zeolites, while famously size-selective, also includes considerations of host properties, such as structurally integral Lewis acid sites,17 and alkali-metal counter-cations, such as those that render the natural zeolite, chabazite, selective for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .18 For MOFs, with much larger host domains and connective channels,19 size selectivity for small molecules is limited, but counterbalanced by other controlling phenomena20 such as smallmolecule (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) condensation, in combination with the chemical properties of the host environment,21,22 the presence of Lewis-acid sites,23,24 and more recently by guest-triggered conformational changes to pore geometries.25 With few exceptions,26 however, including relatively small host systems,1,6,9 the above structures do not inherently provide synthetically reliable options for chemically modifying outer apertures or pores to chemo-selectively differentiate between similarly sized guests.
In this regard, we recently reported27 200-nm gold-nanoparticle (gold-NP) supraspheres28-31 (Figure 1A), prepared by using the metal-oxide cluster-anion ligand, $\mathrm{AlW}_{11}\mathrm{O}_{39}{}^{9-}$ (1) to control the reaction of 4-nm gold NPs with hexanethiol (hex-SH) in water. The reaction results in the hydrophobically driven self-assembly32- 35 of hex-S coated gold cores into close-packed supraspheres featuring interconnected networks of tetrahedral $(T_{\mathrm{d}})$ and octahedral $(O_{\mathrm{h}})$ host domains, and exchangeable molecules of 1 at their outer surfaces (Figure 1A). Each suprasphere serves as a soluble host for over two million27 hydrophobic guests, a capacity rivalling those of zeolites and MOFs. While exploring that phenomenon, we found that hydrophilic polyethylene glycol thiolates (PEG-S) at the outer-pore regions of the suprapheres (lower right in Figure 1A, and Figure 1B) inhibited the uptake of non-polar hydrophobic guests, while uptake of the same guests was observed for supraspheres wrapped by mixtures of hexanethiolate (hex-S) and PEG-S (Figure 1C). Those preliminary findings27 pointed to a new type of ligand-regulated chemoselectivity.
We now report that ligand exchange at the outer pores that provide access to the interior of supraspherical hosts comprised of self-assembled gold-core SBUs makes it possible to discriminate between differently shaped, yet similarly sized and compositionally identical hydrophobic guests. Moreover, guest occupancies vary as a linear function of their dipole moments. This unprecedented phenomenon provides a rational basis for introducing finely-tuned chemoselectivity to host-guest chemistry in water.
Synthesis of the ca. $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ supraspheres27 (Figure 2) relies on the ability of 1 to serve as a self-reporting leaving group36 for controlling the reaction of gold NPs with hex-SH,37 allowing for their hydrophobically driven assembly while continuously imparting solubility in water to the growing assemblies. Remaining molecules of 1 can then be replaced by other ligands, such as PEG-S,27 to give the outer shell illustrated at the upper right in Figure 2 (see Figures S1-S3 in Section S1 of the supporting information). A cryo-TEM images of a suprasphere in its native (vitreous-water) solution state is shown at the lower right in Figure 2.
In the present work, equal amounts of PEG-S wrapped supraspheres were dissolved in $1.5{\mathrm{-mL}}$ aqueous solutions, each saturated with either: $p\textmd{-},o-$ , or $m\cdot$ -, di-chloro or dinitro-benzene, monochloro- or mononitrobenzene. After 7 days, the supraspheres were isolated by centrifugation and dissolved in $\mathrm{CD}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , whereupon the hydrophobic assemblies separated into individual gold NPs,27 simultaneously releasing the aromatic guests, which were quantified by 1NMR spectroscopy (Figures S4-S11, Section S2-S4).
The total host-domain volume within each suprasphere, comprised of $\mathrm{~N~}$ approximately close-packed gold NPs, was calculated as the sum of the volumes of the 2N $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ and N $O_{\mathrm{h}}$ holes. Percent $(\%)$ occupancy of this total volume by the aromatic guests,38 (Table S1) was determined after the seven-day reactions. Occupancy values (Figure 3A) ranged from $0\%$ for both $p$ - host (Table S1). Remarkably, a linear correlation was found between the dipole moments of the eight guests and percent occupancies after the equal reaction times (Figure 3A). Notably, $\%$ -occupancy values showed no correlation with substrate solubility (Figure 3B), which (in principle) could affect uptake rates. Furthermore, previously reported control experiments27 preclude adsorption of the guest within the ca. $10\mathrm{-nm}$ thick PEG layer.
Preference for the nitro- versus chloro-substituted guests was reversed by making the ligand domains around the outer pores partially hydrophobic. This was done by replacing $50\%$ of 1 at the outer surface of the suprasphere by PEG-S, and remaining 1 by hex-S (Figure 4A).27 The "patchy"41 ligand shell (bottom of Figure 4A, and Figure 1C) was suggested by observed values for the uptake of the nitro-substituted guests (Figure 4B).
Notably, if the two ligand types formed a Janus-like (semispherical) ligand shell,42 rates of uptake (occupancies after 7 days) of the more polar (nitro-substituted) guests should be at least half as large as those observed with PEG-S alone. In all cases, however, $\%$ -occupancies decreased by more than $50\%$ , suggesting that the two ligand types formed numerous smaller, partially hydrophobic island-like37 domains around the external pores, as shown in Figure 1C (Figures S12-S18, Section S5).
With 1:1 hex-S:PEG-S, the ligand environment around the access pores is (on average) much less polar. Now, the less polar benzene derivatives (i.e., mono- and dichloro-), with dipole moments ranging from 1.48 to $2.25~D$ , selectively enter the suprasphere host more rapidly (blue squares in Figure 5), giving much larger occupancies than in Figure 3A (for PEG-S only), while entry $\%$ -occupancy) of the more polar nitrobenzenes (red circles in Figure 5) is correspondingly depressed.
