As observed for $100\%$ PEG-S (Figure 3A), occupancies varied as linear functions of dipole moments, in the present case with different responses (and two separate linear plots: Figure 5) for the less polar, chloro-substituted guests (blue squares), and more polar, nitro-substituted guests (red circles).
The linear behaviour observed in Figures 3 and 5 is consistent with steady-state partition of the guests between water and the 1.5 nm (hex-S) to several-nm thick (PEG-S) ligand domains43 in the vicinity of the outer pores, in combination with approximately planar diffusion44 through these self-assembled monolayers. The driving force for uptake itself is provided by hydrophobic interactions with the entirely hex-S coated gold surfaces inside the supraspherical assembly. According to this model, relative rates of guest uptake appear to obey the first-order kinetics typical of diffusion through planar membranes.45,46
In addition to chemoselective uptake, the choice of gold-core size additionally determines the dimensions of the approximately triangular pores that provide entry into the host assembly and "percolation" of guests to interior $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{h}}$ and $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ holes (host domains). This naturally provides options for size-selectivity, analogous to that in porous solid-state materials.
This was demonstrated by preparing supraspheres from hexanethiolate-coated $2\cdot\mathrm{nm}$ diameter gold cores, with average pore heights $(h)$ of $1.47\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 6A; see Figures S19-S24, Section S6). Notably, PEG-S and 1:1 hex-S:PEG-S ligand shells gave the same chemoselectivity as that described above for the 4- nm gold core assemblies (Figures 3-5; Figures S25 –S38, Section S7, Table S2). Using 1:1 hex-S:PEG-S ligand shells, three different molecular-weight polystyrene (PS) spheres were then used as incrementally larger38 guests (center of Figure 6). Due to PS insolubility in water, uptake reactions were carried out in 1:39 water:DMF. Using the $_{4-\mathrm{nm}}$ gold-core assembly $\mathit{\acute{h}}=2.20\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; Figure 6B), PS spheres of all three sizes (i.e., 1.34, 1.62 and 2.14 nm), were found as guests after 7 days, with respective occupancies (decreasing with PS size) of $45\%$ , $18\%$ and $7.6\%$ (Figures S39-S41, Section S8, Table S3). By contrast, using the 2- nm gold-core assembly, only the smaller (1.34 and $1.62\ \mathrm{nm}\$ PS spheres were sequestered, with respective occupancies of $33\%$ and $11.8\%$ (Figures S42-S44, Section S9, Table S4).
Interestingly, non-polar PS spheres penetrated the 1:1 hexS:PEG-S ligand shells, while no uptake was observed for $p$ - dinitrobenzene (Figure 5). A tentative explanation, based on electrostatics, is that even for $p$ -dinitrobenzene (with a dipole moment of zero), the negatively charged O atoms of each nitro group (which sum to a formal charge of $^{-1}$ ) prevent adsorption into hex-S ligand domains, while for $100\%$ PEG-S (Figure 3), the zero dipole moment of $p$ -dinitrobenzene itself dominates, precluding adsorption within the polar polyether structure. By contrast, PS is entirely compatible with hex-S domains. Support for the role of electrostatics is provided by the modest uptake of $p$ -dichlorobenzene by 1:1 hex-S:PEG-S coated supraspheres (Figure 5). Like PS, $p$ -dichlorobenzene is non-polar, and with -Cl rather than $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ substituents, enters the supraspheres through pores located in more highly hex-S-rich domains.
In summary, ligand exchange at the outer surfaces of porous water-soluble assemblies prepared from gold-core structural building units (SBUs) provides inherent options for introducing a type of chemoselectivity not readily achievable with capsules, cages and containers, porous solid-state oxides or metal-organic frameworks. Unprecedented control over chemoselective uptake is demonstrated by a linear correlation between host-domain occupancies at a fixed time and the dipole moments of eight chloro- and nitro-functionalized benzenes (Figure 3), and by reversal of selectivity by simply changing the nature of the ligand shell from PEG-S to 1:1 hex-S:PEG-S (Figure 5). This finely tuned control—understood as a nano-metric scale manifestation of the kinetically first-order diffusion through planar membranes, is readily adaptable to other classes of guests by simply introducing other ligands or ligand combinations, and can be combined with traditional size selectivity by changing the size of the gold-core SBUs. The findings are not only fundamentally important to understanding and quantitatively controlling substrate sequestration, but demonstrate a conceptually new approach, in which membrane-like diffusion through tailored ligand domains is utilized to rationally control and finely tune the chemoselective uptake of similarly-sized guests.
